<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monkwearmouth–Jarrow Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">674</span> · Also known as Wearmouth-Jarrow, Wearmouth–Jarrow</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monkwearmouth–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow">Jarrow</a> Abbey was a twin monastery in Northumbria, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-biscop">Benedict Biscop</a> at Wearmouth in 674 and at Jarrow around 681–82. Biscop brought books, masons and glaziers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and the house became a leading centre of learning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> lived and wrote there, and its scriptorium produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-amiatinus">Codex Amiatinus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> sacked it in the late 8th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monkwearmouth-jarrow-abbey.jpg" alt="Monkwearmouth–Jarrow Abbey" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stanley John James · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Peters_Church_2019_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
